00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.540
Chris: Software should only be released
after it has gone through a proper process

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:07.060
of development, testing and bug fixing.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:12.350
Testing is a critical component of the
development process looking at areas such

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:17.570
as ‘performance’, ‘stability’ and ‘error
handling’ by setting up test scenarios under

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:20.530
controlled conditions and assessing the results.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:26.240
Leah: There are generally four recognized
levels of testing, ‘unit or component

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.400
testing’, ‘integration testing’, ‘system
testing’, and ‘acceptance testing’.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.630
Let’s look at these more closely.

00:00:34.219 --> 00:00:39.430
Chris: Unit Testing is the first level, where
specific lines of code, distinct functionalities,

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:42.970
and desired procedures are isolated and tested.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:48.419
These are termed ‘software units’ because they
are combined to make up the software but are

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.429
also referred to as ‘components’ of the software.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.590
Leah: For example, a line of code in a
“calculator software” can have the function of

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:01.780
implementing ‘+’ “addition,” while another line
of code implements ‘x’ “multiplication,” etc.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:06.979
These and other functions of the calculator
software must be individually tested, to

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.649
ensure that the calculator operates flawlessly.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:14.610
Chris: Integration Testing involves combining
all the components that make up the software,

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:20.330
and testing ‘everything as a whole’,
instead of individually, as in unit testing.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:25.440
From this level, tests can be split into
‘functional’ and ‘non-functional’ types which

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.909
we will look at further later in this session.

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.470
So why is integration testing important?

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:36.840
Leah: A large team of developers working on
a project will be responsible for writing

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:41.699
different parts of the code, which will
require testing to ensure that it is correct.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:47.830
By testing, we can identify and correct
faults such as ‘inter-working defects’,

00:01:48.559 --> 00:01:53.430
‘simultaneous operation defects’, ‘parallel
operation defects’ amongst others.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:59.999
‘Integration testing’ ascertains how well the
units of code work together, and the condition

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.890
of the interfaces between each of them.

00:02:02.599 --> 00:02:07.664
Sometimes, a line of code may work properly
when tested alone, however, when combined with

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:13.010
other lines of code to achieve the desired
function, an error may occur, indicating

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:17.079
that the integration of one or more lines
of code into the rest was unsuccessful.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:22.410
Chris: System testing is carried out to
verify that software operates as required

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:25.110
and is compatible with operating systems.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.380
It tests both the technical and business
logic of the software using ‘functional tests’

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:36.269
on the various ‘functions’ of a system, and
‘non-functional tests’ on how those work.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.580
There have been cases where the Android
version of an application may function

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:43.799
correctly, but the iOS version has issues.

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:49.810
All the software’s completed versions are
tested on all the possible operating systems,

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.660
to ensure all platform functionality.

00:02:53.469 --> 00:02:58.069
A functional test may check if a login feature
responds when the user enters a password,

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.830
and a non-functional test may check how long
it takes to log in after password entry.

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:08.720
Leah: System testing can expose such
errors and allow developers to detect

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:10.530
and correct them before launch.

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:14.110
It also looks at how good the
design of the software is.

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:20.290
The internal code may work fine, but the outward
design might have some operational issues.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:26.510
The overall behaviour of the software is another
aspect that system testing helps to authenticate.

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:32.850
It may be well developed but still malfunctions,
due to the different system specifications.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:39.250
System testing helps identify any abnormal
behaviour and helps organisations to outline

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:41.780
the software’s best system specifications.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:47.639
Chris: Acceptance Testing also referred
to as User Acceptance Testing is similar

00:03:47.639 --> 00:03:52.529
to the system testing, but the test is
carried out by some selected end-users.

00:03:53.289 --> 00:03:57.970
This stage determines if the software is finally
ready to be launched to the general public.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:03.370
The selected users give their various opinions
about the operation of the software letting

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:09.160
the organization know if it meets their diverse
requirements or how it can be improved upon.

00:04:09.889 --> 00:04:14.680
Organisations may conduct acceptance testing
using their own specifics, but ideally

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:19.730
users will be selected from a range of
demographics, different operating systems, and

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:22.470
past experiences of using similar software.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.540
There are two types of ‘Acceptance Testing’
these are ‘Alpha and Beta Testing’.

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:36.310
Leah: Alpha testing is carried out by selected
users, alongside an internal team of developers

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:41.770
who control the testing environment aiming
to mimic realistic conditions during testing.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:47.520
The selected users are given access to the
developer’s platform and allowed to use and

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:50.179
test the developed software or application.

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:55.490
During ‘alpha testing’, minor
errors can still be found and fixed.

00:04:56.910 --> 00:05:01.869
Chris: Beta testing is carried out by selected
users on their own devices and operating systems.

00:05:02.469 --> 00:05:07.079
Here, the finished software is sent to
the selected users, to use and test it

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:09.440
over time and then provide feedback.

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:15.919
At the ‘beta level’, the software is expected
to run perfectly, and meet the end user’s needs.

